  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 130 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nPrecipitable water values look to reach a minimum by around \nWednesday (with mean ensemble values around the 25th to 50th \npercentile of climatology) as flow in the mid-levels becomes more \nzonal. Daytime temperatures gradually warm into midweek, but the \nzonal flow will keep that uptrend gradual and highs look to remain \nin the 80s and low 90s for the area under sunny skies.\n\nAn upper-level trough is forecast to swing from the Four Corners \nregion into West Texas on Thursday, and then over the Edwards \nPlateau / Concho Valley on Friday as it transitions into a closed \nlow. This will bring an increase in moisture and shower/storm \nchances to around 15-30% Thursday and Friday closer to the Rio \nGrande and Edwards Plateau. The GEFS/GFS is the most aggressive with \nthis pattern, depicting a stronger disturbance than most other \nguidance, and consequently higher rain coverage.\n\nSeparate from this feature, a tropical disturbance is forecast to \ndrift across the Gulf, with the National Hurricane Center currently \nindicating a high (70%) chance of development into a tropical \ndepression or storm within the next 7 days. While ensembles diverge \nregarding its potential track, the presence of the approaching \ntrough will imbue strong upper-level wind shear and tend to keep \nmost of its moisture and rainbands out of our area. If the system \ntakes a north or northeastward trajectory shown in most models, the \nimpacts to our area would be very minor. Still, weak easterly flow \noff the Gulf brings a low (less than 25%) chance of a few isolated \nto scattered coastal showers over the Coastal Plains, during the \nsecond half of the week.\n\nThe low rain chances then lessen for the weekend as the trough moves \npast the area. The prevailing upper-level low is expected to turn \nsouthwesterly, supporting warm temperatures and an increase in dew \npoints.",
